====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[travel]] or [[move about]] in an [[aimless]], [[idle]], or [[pretentiously]] [[casual]] way.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Lee Rourke|title=The Canal|publisher=Melville House Publishing|isbn=9781935554905|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=_i4Cba-ZPGQC&pg=PT212&dq=%22He+swans+around+that+stinking+office+in+his+expensive+clothes+that+are+a+little%22|page=unnumbered|text=He '''swans''' around that stinking office in his expensive clothes that are a little too tight for comfort, he '''swans''' around that stinking office without a care in the world.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Tilly Bagshawe|title=One Summer’s Afternoon|publisher=HarperCollins|isbn=9780007472550|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=EOgRAXTEtJYC&pg=PT70&dq=%22swan+off+to+Sardinia+%22|text=One of the few strokes of good luck Emma had had in recent days was the news that Tatiana Flint-Hamilton, her only real rival for top billing as 'most photographable girl' at today's event had decided to '''swan''' off to Sardinia instead, leaving the limelight entirely to Emma.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|author=w:Ling Ma|title=Bliss Montage|chapter=G|location=New York|publisher=Farrar, Straus and Giroux|isbn=978-0-374-71712-4|passage=On the other side: the rich, beautiful tapestry of WASP culture that constituted Levis's life—friends playing horseshoes at backyard cocktail parties, where girls '''swanned''' in chaise longues, clinking their gin and tonics.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2026 April 1|author=Christian Wolmar|title=Managers must be given freedom to make decisions|journal=RAIL|issue=1058|page=35|text=He developed the Capitalcard, which combined Tube and train travel - a return fare from a NSE station would include free Tube travel for the day after 0930, with the slogan ''''Swanning''' around London' on posters featuring the bird.}}  

=====Usage notes=====
* In the sense “to travel”, usually used as part of the phrase “to [[swan about]]” or “to swan around”.

===Etymology 2===
Probably from dialectal {{m|en|I s’wan}}, a corruption of ''I shall warrant''; or possibly from a minced form of {{m|en|I [[swear]] on}}.

====Alternative forms====
* [[swanny]]

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|US|dialectal|or|colloquial}} To [[declare]] (chiefly in first-person present constructions).
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1907|month=December|author=J. D. Archer|title=Foiling an eavesdropper|journal=Telephony|volume=14|page=345
|passage="Well, I '''swan''', man, I had a better opinion of you than that."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1940|author=w:Raymond Chandler|title=Farewell, My Lovely|page=214|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2010
|passage=‘She slammed the door so hard I figured a window'd break {{...}}.’ ‘I '''swan''',’ I said.}}

