===Noun===
{{en-noun|es|syntaces|syntices<l:proscribed>}}

# A set of rules that govern how words are combined to form [[phrase]]s and [[sentence]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1988|author=Andrew Radford|title=Transformational grammar: a first course|location=Cambridge, UK|publisher=Cambridge University Press|page=410|chapter=8
|passage=&nbsp;&nbsp;The incorporation of a rule of V <small>MOVEMENT</small> into our description of English '''Syntax''' turns out to have fundamental theoretical implications for our overall Theory of Grammar: it means that we are no longer able to posit that the syntactic structure of a sentence can be described in terms of a single Phrase-marker representing its S-structure. For, the postulation of a rule of V-<small>MOVEMENT</small> means that we must recognise at least two different levels of structure in our Theory of Grammar — namely, a level of ''D-structure'' (formerly known as ‘Deep Structureʼ) which serves as input to the rule, and a separate level of ''S-structure'' which is formed by application of the rule.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|countable}} The formal rules of formulating the [[statements]] of a computer language.
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} The study of the structure of phrases, sentences, and language.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Richard Nordquist|title=What Is Headlinese?|magazine=w:ThoughtCo|url=https://www.thoughtco.com/what-is-headlinese-1690921|date=2021-05-29|passage={{q-g|Otto}} Jespersen, a language professor known for his expertise in '''syntax''' and language development, stated that headlinese is not really grammatical writing.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20211210084548/https://www.thoughtco.com/what-is-headlinese-1690921}}

