====Verb====
{{en-verb|~s|~ming|swam,swum<l:archaic>|swum}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[move]] through the [[water]], without touching the bottom; to [[propel]] [[oneself]] in [[water]] by [[natural]] means.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1720|author=w:Daniel Defoe|title=w:Captain Singleton|location=London|publisher=J. Brotherton|page=87|url=https://archive.org/details/lifeadventurespy1720defo
|passage=We were now all upon a Level, as to our travelling; being unshipp’d, for our Bark would '''swim''' no farther, and she was too heavy to carry on our Backs {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=Foreword|passage=He turned back to the scene before him and the enormous new block of council dwellings. The design was some way after Corbusier but the block was built up on plinths and resembled an Atlantic liner '''swimming''' diagonally across the site.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 May 20|author=Paul Stephen|title=NR beats floods to secure tracks to Drax|journal=Rail|page=59|text=Meanwhile, NR faced an unexpected challenge when a night watchman spotted several Koi Carp '''swimming''' in floodwater close to the railway, after they had escaped from a nearby private residence. Wilson says the owner was eventually traced, and the fish were safely returned "after enjoying '''swimming''' around in a 3,000-acre lake".}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become immersed in, or as if in, or flooded with, or as if with, a liquid.
#: {{co|en|'''swimming''' in self-pity}}
#: {{ux|en|a bare few bits of meat '''swimming''' in watery sauce}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move around freely because of excess space.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1777|title=The Poetical Preceptor; Or, a Collection of Select Pieces of Poetry, Etc
|passage=A fam'd Sur-tout he wears, which ''once'' was blue, <br> And his foot '''swims''' in a capacious shoe.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[traverse]] (a specific body of water, or a specific distance) by swimming; or, to use a specific swimming [[stroke]]; or, to compete in a specific swimming event.
#: {{ux|en|For exercise, we like to '''swim''' laps around the pool.}}
#: {{ux|en|I want to '''swim''' the 200-yard breaststroke in the finals.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|10|text=Sometimes he thought to '''swim''' the stormy main.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|uncommon}} To cause to swim.
#: {{ux|en|to '''swim''' a horse across a river}}
#: {{ux|en|Half of the guinea pigs were '''swum''' daily.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} To [[float]].
#: {{co|en|[[sink or swim|sink or '''swim''']]}}
#: {{ux|en|There's oil '''swim'''ming on the water.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|V|1|text=Why, now, blow wind, swell billow, and '''swim''' bark!<br>The storm is up and all is on the hazard.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|2 Kings|6|6|text=And he cut down a stick, and cast it in thither; and the iron did '''swim'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be overflowed or drenched.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Psalms|6|6|text=I am weary with my groaning; all the night make I my bed to swim; I water my couch with my tears.}}
#* {{RQ:Thomson Seasons|year=1768|page=127|line=336|passage=Sudden, the ditches ſwell; the meadows '''ſwim'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[immerse]] in water to make the lighter parts float.
#: {{ux|en|to '''swim''' wheat in order to select seed}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|historical}} To test (a suspected [[witch]]) by throwing into a [[river]]; those who [[float]]ed rather than [[sink]]ing were deemed to be witches.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[glide]] along with a waving motion.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have a great quantity of something.
#: {{co|en|You should be '''swimming''' in money after this job.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In Late Middle English and Early Modern English, the present participle form '''swimmand''' still sometimes occurred in Midlands and Northern dialects, for exampleː
** ''The water to [[nourish|norish]] the [[fish|fysh]] '''swymand'''.'' (14th c., ''The Creation'' in The Towneley plays, l. 55)
** ''Their young child Troiane, as swift as dolphin fish, '''swimmand''' away.'' (1513, Gavin Douglas, Virgil's Aeneid)

