===Etymology===
Early attestations (starting in 1624)<ref name="MWO">{{R:MWO}}</ref> are from North America,<ref name="NED">{{R:New English Dictionary}}</ref> but the term was probably in local use in Britain earlier.<ref name="NED"/> The etymology is not entirely certain; it is probably a fusion of {{inh|en|enm|swam|t=swamp, muddy pool, bog, marsh”, also “fungus, mushroom}} — from {{inh|en|ang|swamm|t=mushroom, fungus, sponge}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*swamm}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*swammaz|*swambaz, *swammaz}} — and {{inh|en|enm|sompe|t=marsh, morass}}, from either {{der|en|dum|somp}}, {{m|dum|sump|t=marsh, swamp}} or {{der|en|gml|sump|t=marsh, swamp}} (from {{der|en|osx|*sump|t=swamp, marsh}}), both from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*sump}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*sumpaz}}. *''Swambaz'', *''swammaz'' and *''sumpaz'' are likely related to each other, but it is unclear whether they are of Indo-European origin or are substrate words or wanderworts.<ref>{{R:nl:Etymologiebank|zomp2}}</ref>

The word has alternatively been suggested to be a borrowing from {{cog|nl|zwamp|t=swamp, marsh, fen}}.<ref name="D">{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref> Other cognates include {{cog|gml|swamp|t=sponge, mushroom}}, {{cog|nl|zomp|t=swamp, lake, marshy place}}, {{cog|nds-de|Sump|t=swamp, bog,
marsh}}, {{cog|de|Sumpf|t=swamp}}, {{cog|sv|sump|t=swamp}}. Related also to {{cog|nl|zwam|t=fungus, punk, tinder}}, {{cog|de|Schwamm|t=mushroom, fungus, sponge}}, {{cog|sv|svamp|t=mushroom, fungus, sponge}}, {{cog|is|svampur}}, {{m|is|sveppur|t=fungus}}, {{cog|got|𐍃𐍅𐌿𐌼𐍃𐌻|t=a ditch}}. Related to {{l|en|sump}}, {{l|en|swim}}.

