===Etymology 1===
The {{glossary|adjective}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|tauni}}, {{m|enm|tawne|t=having a brownish-orange colour}}{{nb...|taunde, taunei, tauneʒ, tauny, tawny, (chiefly Northern England, Scotland) tanei, tani, tanne, tannee, tanny, tannye, tany, thanne|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=
taunī|pos=adj|id=MED44608}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|taune}}, {{m|xno|tawné}}, and {{der|en|fro|tané}}, {{m|fro|tanné}}, {{m|fro|tanney|t=of a tan colour}}, an adjective use of the {{glossary|past}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|fro|taner|t=to turn hide into leather, tan}}, from {{m|fro|tan|t=pulped oak bark used to tan leather, tanbark}},<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=adj.'' and ''n|id=198256|date=March 2022}}</ref><ref>{{R:Lexico|pos=adj}}</ref><ref>{{R:Dictionary.com|pos=adj|source=Random House}}</ref> ultimately from {{der|en|cel-pro|*tannos|t=green oak}};<ref>{{R:EDPC|369|head=*tanno-}}</ref> further etymology uncertain, possibly from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)dʰnwos}}, {{m|ine-pro|*(s)dʰonu|t=fir}}.

The ''-aw-'' spelling (also ''-au-'' in Middle English) seems to have been due to the pronunciation of {{cog|fro|tané}}.<ref name="OED"/>

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the adjective.<ref name="OED"/>

