#: {{synonyms|en|fulvid|fulvous|olivaster|subfuscous|swart|swartish|swarty|Thesaurus:brownish}}
#* {{RQ:Holinshed Chronicles|volume=II|chapter=[[w:Henry V of England|King Henrie the Fifth]]|page=1077|column=2|passage=And if any of your nation attempte once to ſtoppe me in my iorney now towards Calais, {{...}} I in my defence ſhall colour and make red your '''tawny''' ground with the effuſion of chriſtian bloud: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer Q1|act=III|scene=i|page=29|passage=The [[ouzel|VVooſell]] cock, ſo blacke of hevve, / VVith Orange '''tavvny''' bill, / The Throſtle, vvith his note ſo true, / The VVren, vvith little quill.}}
#* {{RQ:Jonson Poetaster|scene=iv|page=56|passage=Come, vve muſt haue you turne Fiddler againe, ſlaue, 'get a Baſe Violin at your backe, and march in a '''Tavvnie''' Coate, vvith one ſleeue, to Gooſe-faire, and then you'll knovve vs; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe New Voyage|part=I|page=155|passage=To the Queen he gave {{...}} a ſmall Box full of large Needles; then he gave her ſome courſe brovvn Thread, and ſhovv'd her hovv to thred the Needle and ſovv any Thing together vvith the Thread; all vvhich ſhe admired exceedingly, and call'd her '''Tavvny''' Maids of Honour about her, that they might learn alſo.}}
#* {{RQ:Cowper Homer|volume=I|book=XI|page=289|lines=661–662|passage=[T]he vvatch-dogs and aſſembled ſvvains / Have driv'n a '''tavvny''' lion from the ſtalls, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Martin Chuzzlewit|chapter=Will be Seen in the Long Run, if Not in the Short One, to Concern Mr. Pinch and Others, Nearly.{{nb...|Mr. Pecksniff Asserts the Dignity of Outraged Virtue; and Young Martin Chuzzlewit Forms a Desparate Resolution.}}|pages=150–151|pageref=151|passage=[T]he head waiter inquired with respectful solicitude whether that port, being a light and '''tawny''' wine, was suited to his taste, or whether he would wish to try a fruity port with greater body.}}
#* {{RQ:Eliot Adam Bede|volume=I|chapter=In the Wood|page=240|passage=[P]erhaps they metamorphose themselves into a '''tawny''' squirrel that scampers away and mocks you from the topmost bough.}}
#* {{RQ:Thoreau Cape Cod|page=14|passage=There were the '''tawny''' rocks, like lions couchant, defying the ocean, whose waves incessantly dashed against and scoured them with vast quantities of gravel.}}
#* {{RQ:Noyes Poems|poem=The Highwayman|part=2|stanza=I|pages=48–49|pageref=48|passage=He did not come in the dawning; he did not come at noon; / And out o' the '''tawny''' sunset, before the rise o' the moon, / When the road was a gypsy's ribbon, looping the purple moor, / A red-coat troop came marching— / Marching—marching— / King George's men came marching, up to the old inn-door.}}
#* {{RQ:Grahame Wind in the Willows|chapter=Wayfarers All|page=196|passage=They fell a-twittering among themselves once more, and this time their intoxicating babble was of violet seas, '''tawny''' sands, and lizard-haunted walls.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=Frank Maudsley; [[w:Paul Reynolds (musician)|Paul Reynolds]]; Ali Score; w:Mike Score|artist=w:A Flock of Seagulls|title=w:I Ran (So Far Away)|album=[[w:A Flock of Seagulls (album)|A Flock of Seagulls]]|month=March|year=1982|passage=I never thought I'd meet a girl like you / With auburn hair and '''tawny''' eyes}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Cannon (historian)|John Cannon]]; Anne Hargreaves|chapter=Romano-British Rulers|title=The Kings & Queens of Britain|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|year=2001|page=13|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/kingsqueensofbri0000cann_j0y7/page/13/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-19-280095-4|passage=[[w:Cassius Dio|Dio Cassius]], writing more than one hundred years after the event, described [[w:Boudica|Boudicca]] as 'very tall, in appearance most terrifying&nbsp;… the glance of her eye most fierce, her voice harsh&nbsp;… a great mass of the '''tawniest''' hair fell to her hips'.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Roger Tory Peterson; Michael DiGiorgio; Paul Lehman; Peter Pyle; Larry Rosche|chapter=Owls and Nightjars|title=Peterson Field Guide to Birds of North America|series=Peterson Field Guides|edition=2nd|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=w:Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|year=2019|page=230|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=0xCiDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA230|isbn=978-1-328-77144-5|passage=ANTILLEAN NIGHTHAWK ''Chordeiles gundlachii'' {{...}} Somewhat '''tawnier''' and smaller than Common Nighthawk, but readily distinguished from it only by call.}}

