# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To fly or glide downwards suddenly; to [[plunge]] (in the air) or [[nosedive]].
#: {{syn|en|stoop}}
#: {{ux|en|The lone eagle '''swooped''' down into the lake, snatching its prey, a small fish.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move swiftly, as if with a sweeping movement, especially to attack something.
#: {{ux|en|The dog had enthusiastically '''swooped''' down on the bone.}}
#* {{RQ:Williams Velveteen Rabbit|passage=There was a person called Nana who ruled the nursery. Sometimes she took no notice of the playthings lying about, and sometimes, for no reason whatever, she went '''swooping''' about like a great wind and hustled them away in cupboards.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 January 12|author=Howard Johnston|title=Regional News: Scotland|journal=RAIL|issue=948|page=19|text=Bridge of Weir: Protection of the site of the former Kilmacolm branch station (closed on January 3 1983) has been lifted, and developers have '''swooped''' in with plans for new housing.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To fall on at once and seize; to catch while on the wing.
#* Quoted in '''1971''', ''The Scriblerian'' (volumes 4-5, page 2)
#*: And his Eagles, which can with the same ease as a kite '''swoops''' a chicken, snatch up a strong built Chamber of wood 12 foot square, & well crampt & fortified with Iron, with all its furniture, & a man besides, & carry it to the Clouds?
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To seize; to catch up; to take with a sweep.
#* {{RQ:Glanvill Vanity of Dogmatizing|chapter=An Apology for Philosophy|page=247|passage=Thus the ''Phyſitian'' looks with another Eye on the ''Medicinal hearb'', then the ''grazing Oxe'', which '''ſwoops''' it in with the common ''graſs'': {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1670|author=w:John Dryden|title=w:The Conquest of Granada
|passage=And now at last you come to '''swoop''' it all.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To pass with pomp; to [[sweep]].
#* {{RQ:Drayton Poly-Olbion|page=6|passage=Proude ''Tamer'' '''swoopes''' along, with such a lustie traine / As fits so brave a flood two Countries that divides: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|Britain|prison slang}} To search the ground for discarded [[cigarette]] [[butt]]s that can be made into new cigarettes.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1989|author=Michael Bettsworth|title=Marking Time: A Prison Memoir|page=32
|passage=He was forever diving into dustbins or '''swooping''' on to the ground for cigarette ends.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2015|author=Noel 'Razor' Smith|title=The Criminal Alphabet: An A-Z of Prison Slang
|passage='''Swooping''' is picking up discarded cigarette butts from the exercise yard and anywhere else they can be found.}}

