===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|suprema}}

# {{lb|en|set theory}} {{lb|en|real analysis}}: Given a [[subset]] ''X'' of '''R''', the smallest [[real number]] that is ≥ every element of ''X''; {{lb|en|order theory}}: given a [[subset]] ''X'' of a [[partially ordered set]] ''P'' (with [[partial order]] ≤), the [[least]] element ''y'' of ''P'' such that every element of ''X'' is ≤ ''y''.
#: {{syn|en|least upper bound|LUB|sup}}
#: {{cot|en|infimum}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Charalambos D. Aliprantis; Kim C. Border|title=Infinite Dimensional Analysis: A Hitchhiker's Guide|edition=3rd|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=4hIq6ExH7NoC&pg=PA8&dq=%22supremum%22%7C%22suprema%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiKnK-l4vXbAhXLFJQKHRD1CPEQ6AEIdjAQ#v=onepage&q=%22supremum%22%7C%22suprema%22&f=false|page=8|publisher=Springer|passage=A '''sublattice''' of a lattice is a subset that is closed under pairwise infima and '''suprema'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=James S. Howland|title=Basic Real Analysis|publisher={{w|Jones & Bartlett Learning|Jones & Bartlett Publishers}}|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=zyNXBggk8GEC&pg=PA9&dq=%22supremum%22%7C%22suprema%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiKnK-l4vXbAhXLFJQKHRD1CPEQ6AEIMTAB#v=onepage&q=%22supremum%22%7C%22suprema%22&f=false|page=9|passage=The best way to describe the '''supremum''' of ''S'' is to say that it wants to be the greatest element of ''S''. In fact, if ''S'' has a greatest element, then that element is the '''supremum'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Andreas Löhne|title=Vector Optimization with Infimum and '''Supremum'''|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=gvbcBIFp3xEC&pg=PR7&dq=%22supremum%22%7C%22suprema%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiKnK-l4vXbAhXLFJQKHRD1CPEQ6AEINzAC#v=onepage&q=%22supremum%22%7C%22suprema%22&f=false|page=vii|publisher=Springer|passage=The key to an approach to vector optimization based on infimum and '''supremum''' is to consider set-based objective functions and to extend the partial ordering of the original objective space to a suitable subspace of the power set. In this new space the infimum and '''supremum''' exist under the usual assumptions.}}

====Usage notes====
* Commonly denoted sup(''X'').
* The supremum of ''X'' may not exist, and, if it does, may not be an element of ''X''.
* {{sense|order theory}}
** Formally: Let <math>S =\{ t : t \in P : \forall x\in X, x \le t \}</math> be the set of [[upper]] [[bound]]s of ''X''. Then sup(''X''), if it exists, is the element <math>s\in S: \forall y\in S, s \le y</math>.
** The concept of ''supremum'' is closely related to the function [[∨]] (called {{m|en|join}}). The supremum of two elements, denoted <math>\sup\{x,y\}</math> can also be written <math>x\lor y</math>. The supremum of a set may be denoted <math>\sup(X)</math> or <math>\bigvee X</math>.

